<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.15.4: Identifying Graph Regions Included in a System’s Solution</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00655</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.15.4: Identifying Graph Regions Included in a System’s Solution</md:title>
    <md:uuid>3fb9b7ee-e538-4edc-bd20-3226797db471</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section>
<div class="os-raise-familysupport">
  <para>Access the <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b62c9150250bf52d6cc37569213ab516e0090dca">Desmos guide PDF</link> for tips on solving problems with the Desmos graphing calculator.</para>
</div>

<para>Use this information to answer questions 1 – 7.
</para>
<blockquote>
  <para>A factory produces “widgets” and “zurls.” The combined number of widgets and zurls made each day cannot be more than 12. The maximum number of widgets the factory can produce in a day is 4.</para>
  <para>Let \(x\) be the number of widgets and \(y\) be the number of zurls.</para>
</blockquote>
<!--BEGIN ACTIVITY MULTISELECTION 1 TRY -->
<section class="check-understanding"><title>Cool Down Activity</title><exercise data-content-id="098f8697-a574-4da1-a25e-310a94269385"><problem><para>
      <para>Select <emphasis>two</emphasis> inequalities that represent this situation. </para>
      </para></problem><solution><para>["\\(x≤4\\)", "\\(x+y≤12\\)"]</para><para>
      <para>Nice try! The correct answers are \(x\leq4\) and \(x+y\leq12\)</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><!--END ACTIVITY MULTISELECTION 1 TRY -->
<!--BEGIN ADD DESMOS INTERATIVE-->
<!--START QUESTION #2 -->    
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
      <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the equations \(x=4\) and \(x+y=12\) using the Desmos tool below. </item>
    </list>

<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
 
    <!--QUESTION IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS--> 
  <para>When you have finished graphing, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <figure><media alt="Graph of a line, origin O. X axis from 0 to 12 by 2’s. Y axis from 0 to 12 by 2’s. Line passes through 0 comma 12, 2 comma 10, 4 comma 8, 6 comma 6, 8 comma 4, 10 comma 2 and 12 comma 0.  "><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c139281acbcf5a56cdc0953109560629168ca9a6"/></media></figure></solution></exercise>
</example><div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>
<!--END ADD DESMOS INTERATIVE-->
<!--BEGIN ADD DESMOS INTERATIVE-->
<!--START QUESTION #3 -->    
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
      <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course.  Graph the system of inequalities that shows all the allowable numbers of widgets and zurls that the factory can produce in one day using the Desmos tool below.</item>
    </list>

<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
 
    <!--QUESTION IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS--> 
  <para>When you have finished graphing, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <figure><media alt="Two inequalities on a coordinate plane  "><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a20ed44fb6a7d9bcc05e1875ebb23da25d3b157c"/></media></figure></solution></exercise>
</example><div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>
<!--END ADD DESMOS INTERATIVE-->

<para>In questions 4 – 7 answer the question:
</para><blockquote>Does the ordered pair represent an allowable combination of widgets and zurls produced in one day?</blockquote>

<!-- BEGIN ACTIVITY DROP DOWN 1 TRY -->
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="6347f36e-02cd-47ac-8f39-15175e49b066"><problem><para>
      <para>(4, 5)</para>
      </para></problem><solution><para>Yes</para><para>
      <para>Nice try! The correct answer is Yes.</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><!--END ACTIVITY DROP DOWN 1 TRY --></section>
